_info() {
    cat <<'EOF'
>>> sigrok-bridge -- Sipeed SLogic <-> ngscopeclient bridge

1) USB access
   Installed: /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-sigrok-slogic.rules
   Replug the SLogic (or run the following) so the rule takes effect:
       sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger

2) Start the bridge -- both units bind TCP 10101, enable only one:
   digital (default; every channel is an independent digital line):
       sudo systemctl enable --now sigrok-bridge.service
   analog (8 digital lines -> one 8-bit ADC channel; needs the ADC module):
       sudo systemctl enable --now sigrok-bridge-analog.service

3) Connect from ngscopeclient
   File -> Add -> Oscilloscope
       Driver:    sigrok
       Transport: twinlan
       Path:      localhost:10101      (or <remote-ip>:10101)

Notes
   - Verbose logs: RUST_LOG=debug /usr/bin/sigrok-bridge
   - The bridge exits when no SLogic is present; the service retries
     automatically until the device shows up.
   - SECURITY: the bridge offers no authentication or encryption and listens
     on all interfaces. Only enable it on a trusted network.
EOF
}

_firewall_info() {
    cat <<'EOF'

>>> Firewall: TWO ports are required

   The twinlan transport uses a separate connection for the waveform stream.
   When only the command port is reachable the symptom is misleading: the
   instrument connects "successfully" but the channel list stays empty and no
   waveform is ever drawn.

       tcp/10101  command port   (what you put in Path)
       tcp/10102  data port      = command port + 1

   The data port is ALWAYS the command port + 1. If you run the bridge with a
   custom port (e.g. --port 20101), open that port AND the next one (20102)
   instead of 10101/10102. You can also pin both explicitly from the client by
   using "host:cmdport:dataport", e.g. Path = localhost:10101:10102.

   This only matters when the SLogic is attached to ANOTHER machine; a purely
   local setup (localhost) needs no firewall change.

   firewalld (a service definition is installed):
       sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=sigrok-bridge
       sudo firewall-cmd --reload

   ufw (an application profile is installed):
       sudo ufw allow sigrok-bridge

   nftables:
       sudo nft add rule inet filter input tcp dport '{ 10101, 10102 }' accept

   iptables:
       sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp -m multiport --dports 10101,10102 -j ACCEPT

   To verify both ports are really listening while the bridge runs:
       ss -tlnp | grep sigrok-bridge
EOF
}

post_install() {
    _info
    _firewall_info
}

post_upgrade() {
    _info
    _firewall_info
}
